    appliance: 'Dryer',
    question: 'What do the d80, d90, and d95 codes mean on my LG dryer?',
    metaTitle: 'LG Dryer d80 / d90 / d95 Code? Meaning & Fix — Real Technician',
    metaDesc: 'd80, d90, and d95 on an LG dryer mean the exhaust vent is 80–95% blocked. It\'s a clogged vent — a fire risk. Here\'s what to do and when to call.',
    intro: 'd80, d90, and d95 on an LG dryer are not a broken dryer — they\'re the dryer telling you the exhaust duct is badly restricted: 80%, 90%, or 95% blocked. LG built this warning in on purpose, because a clogged dryer vent is the #1 cause of dryer fires. The dryer is fine; the vent line running to the outside of your house needs to be cleared. Here\'s what it means and how to handle it.',
    safety: '⚠️ A restricted dryer vent is a real fire hazard — lint is highly flammable and heat backs up when it can\'t escape. If you\'re getting d90 or d95, stop running long loads until the vent is cleared. This is the code we most recommend acting on quickly.',
    causes: [
      { name: 'Clogged exhaust vent run (most common)', why: 'Lint builds up over months and years inside the flexible duct behind the dryer and the rigid vent line running through the wall to the outside. As it narrows, airflow drops and the dryer throws d80 → d90 → d95 as it gets worse.', diy: 'Clean the lint trap every load, then check and clear the flexible duct behind the dryer. The full vent run to the exterior usually needs a proper cleaning — this is our dryer-vent cleaning service, and it clears the code and removes the fire risk.', difficulty: 'Moderate' },
      { name: 'Crushed or kinked transition duct', why: 'The flexible duct behind the dryer gets crushed when the dryer is pushed back against the wall, choking airflow even if the rest of the run is clean.', diy: 'Pull the dryer out a few inches and make sure the duct behind it isn\'t crushed flat or kinked. Give it room.', difficulty: 'Easy' },
      { name: 'Blocked or stuck exterior vent flap', why: 'The vent hood on the outside of the house can get clogged with lint, or its flap can stick shut or get blocked by a bird nest, trapping the air.', diy: 'Go outside, find the dryer vent hood, and check that the flap opens freely and isn\'t packed with lint or debris.', difficulty: 'Easy' },
      { name: 'Long or poorly-routed vent line', why: 'A very long run with lots of elbows, or an undersized/ribbed line, restricts airflow enough to trigger the code even when reasonably clean.', diy: 'If the code keeps returning after cleaning, the vent routing itself may need improving — a tech can assess and reroute or upgrade the line.', difficulty: 'Moderate' },
    ],
    repairReplace: 'This is a vent-cleaning job, not a dryer repair — the dryer is working correctly by warning you. A professional dryer-vent cleaning clears the code, restores drying speed, and removes the fire risk. We do this as a dedicated service; ask about it when you book.',
    faqs: [
      { q: 'Is the d80 / d90 / d95 code dangerous?', a: 'It\'s a warning that your dryer vent is 80–95% blocked, which is a genuine fire risk — clogged vents are the leading cause of dryer fires. It also means clothes take forever to dry. Clear the vent promptly, especially at d90 or d95.' },
      { q: 'How do I clear the d90 code on my LG dryer?', a: 'The code clears once the exhaust vent is cleaned. Clean the lint trap, pull the dryer out and check the duct behind it, check the exterior vent flap, and have the full vent run professionally cleaned. It\'s not a part failure — it\'s airflow.' },
      { q: 'Do I need a technician for an LG dryer d80 code?', a: 'You can start by cleaning the lint trap and checking the duct behind the dryer, but the full vent run to the outside usually needs a professional cleaning to fully clear d90/d95 and remove the fire hazard. That\'s exactly what our dryer-vent cleaning covers.' },
    ],
