    slug: 'bosch-dishwasher-e15-error-code',
    appliance: 'Dishwasher',
    question: 'What does the E15 error code mean on my Bosch dishwasher?',
    metaTitle: 'Bosch Dishwasher E15 Code? Meaning & Fix From a Real Technician',
    metaDesc: 'E15 on a Bosch dishwasher means water in the base pan tripped the Aquastop leak protection. Here\'s how to reset it and find the leak — real tech advice.',
    intro: 'E15 on a Bosch dishwasher means water collected in the base pan and tripped the Aquastop leak-protection float — the dishwasher shut itself off to prevent a flood, and it often runs the drain pump nonstop until the base is dry. It\'s a smart safety feature, and sometimes it clears with a simple reset, but it\'s usually telling you water got somewhere it shouldn\'t. Here\'s what a tech does.',
    safety: 'Low-risk to try the reset, but E15 means water has leaked into the base — if it keeps returning, there\'s an active leak that needs finding before it causes floor or cabinet damage. Turn off the water supply if it\'s leaking freely.',
    causes: [
      { name: 'Water pooled in the base pan (reset)', why: 'A one-time overfill, a heavy suds event, or a small splash can trip the float. Often the fix is just drying the base so the float drops back down.', diy: 'Tip the dishwasher back gently (or prop the front up) to drain water out of the base pan, let it dry, and set it upright. Then run a short cycle — if E15 is gone and doesn\'t return, it was a one-time trip.', difficulty: 'Moderate' },
      { name: 'Detergent / rinse-aid overdose (excess suds)', why: 'Too much detergent or the wrong kind creates suds that overflow into the base and trip the float.', diy: 'Use the correct dishwasher detergent and amount; run a rinse cycle to clear existing suds after drying the base.', difficulty: 'Easy' },
      { name: 'An actual leak (hose, pump seal, tub)', why: 'If E15 comes right back after drying the base, water is actively leaking in — from a hose connection, the pump seal, the spray arm supply, or a tub crack.', diy: 'This is the tech diagnosis — finding and fixing the leak source is what clears E15 for good.', difficulty: 'Moderate' },
      { name: 'Stuck float switch', why: 'The Aquastop float itself can stick up (from debris) and report water that isn\'t there.', diy: 'After drying the base, make sure the small foam float in the base moves freely; a stuck float is a tech fix.', difficulty: 'Moderate' },
    ],
    repairReplace: 'Repair — a reset is free, and even a leak fix (a hose, a seal) is far below the cost of a new dishwasher. The important thing is not to ignore a returning E15, since it means water is getting loose.',
    faqs: [
      { q: 'How do I reset the E15 code on my Bosch dishwasher?', a: 'Tip the dishwasher back to drain water out of the base pan and let it dry so the Aquastop float drops, then set it upright and run a short cycle. If E15 is gone, it was a one-time trip. If it returns, there\'s an active leak to find.' },
      { q: 'Why does my Bosch dishwasher keep showing E15?', a: 'A returning E15 means water is actively leaking into the base — a hose, pump seal, spray-arm supply, or tub. That needs a tech to locate and fix the leak; drying the base only resets it temporarily.' },
      { q: 'How much does it cost to fix a Bosch E15 code?', a: 'Often free (a reset after drying the base) or an affordable hose/seal if there\'s a leak. Your $50 Quick Check credits toward the repair if a part is needed.' },
    ],
